\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dlr Higher Late On US Dlr Weakness; MAS  \highlight22\uc2 Intervened \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 453 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Mai 2009\par 08:48\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD              1.4473   -0.0091 \par    Overnight rate        0.25%   -13 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  0.46%    Unchanged \par    10-year bond yield:   2.28%   +7 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  1.46%   -5 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   2.75%   -2 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   129 bps +3 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was higher in Asian trade late Friday, as broad U.S. dollar weakness continued to push the local \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  higher.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar traded as low as S$1.4451, the weakest it has been since Jan. 2, as it continued to fall against the euro amid speculation of potential cuts in the U.S. credit ratings after \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  Ratings Service warned overnight that it might downgrade the U.K.'s triple-A rating.\par \par \uc2 "We question whether the wholesale sell (U.S. dollar) story isn't just a little lazy, though for now momentum is clearly with the trade," \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2  said in a report. "In Asia, double-digit GDP contractions in Japan, Singapore and Taiwan are being shrugged off and reasoned with a view that 'things couldn't possibly get worse' so that must mark the low point."\par \par \uc2 The report added that the Singapore dollar is inappropriately strong on a nominal effective exchange rate basis.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  was seen \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday in the market in order to restrain the local dollar's rise.\par \par \uc2 A person familiar with the situation told Dow Jones Newswires Thursday that the authority would prefer the U.S. dollar to trade above S$1.45, and that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively should it fall convincingly below that mark.\par \par \uc2 "The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's too strong; the fundamentals don't support it," a strategist with a foreign bank said. "MAS should move sooner than later; they'll be way behind the curve if they wait too long."\par \par \uc2 But the person familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities' thinking also said it was questionable whether an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would succeed in defending the U.S. dollar, given its broad-based declines in recent sessions.\par \par \uc2 Local government bonds were mixed, with the 10-year yields remaining under selling pressure as players adjust to a bigger-than-expected issue size of S$2 billion in 10-year notes to be auctioned off next week.\par \par \uc2 A trader with a foreign bank said the market had been expecting around S$1.5 billion, given the relatively modest issue sizes in previous auctions so far this year.\par \par \uc2 -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4155; vincent.lee@dowjones.com [ 22-05-09 0648GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090522e55m0006r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
