\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Singapore April Exports Fall At Faster Pace\b0\par\par\uc2 642 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 Mai 2009\par 09:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more details, background, analysts' comments)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's key exports fell for a 12th straight month in April - and at an accelerated pace - underscoring how fragile the economic crisis has left the island's main trading partners.\par \par \uc2 Non-oil domestic exports declined 19.2% in April from the same month a year earlier and compared with a revised 17.3% drop in March, the trade promotion agency International Enterprise Singapore said Monday.\par \par \uc2 Eight economists polled by Dow Jones Newswires had forecast an 18.6% decline from April a year ago.\par \par \uc2 Tiny Singapore, which is highly dependent on external demand, is suffering its worst-ever recession.\par \par \uc2 Significantly, exports to China, which rose 14% in March and 8% in February, turned back down in April, falling 15% from the same month in 2008.\par \par \uc2 All eyes are on China and the pace at which it imports components from Asian neighbors, only to assemble them into products which it exports abroad, largely to the West.\par \par \uc2 "There is lot of uncertainty still hanging over (global demand)," said David Cohen of Action Economics. He expects the second half of 2009 to usher in a slight recovery in Singapore's exports.\par \par \uc2 According to the data, exports to all of Singapore's top 10 markets fell in April; the E.U., U.S. and Indonesia led the decline.\par \par \uc2 Exports to the U.S. - Singapore's largest export market - fell 35% from a year earlier after declining 31% in March. Shipments to the European Union fell 31% on year compared with a 24% decline the previous month.\par \par \uc2 Electronics exports to China fell 21% on year in April, mainly due to weaker demand for disk drives and personal computer components.\par \par \uc2 Overall electronics exports declined 25.6% in April from a year earlier on reduced shipments of personal computer parts, disc drives and semiconductors. Non-electronics exports slid 14.8% on year as exports of petrochemicals and disk media products declined.\par \par \uc2 Shipments to Japan fell 34% on year in April after dropping 33% the previous month; exports to Indonesia fell 33% in April compared with the same month in 2008.\par \par \uc2 "Perhaps the worst of the contraction may be behind us, but in terms of the headline numbers it's not really looking like we're on track for any meaningful V-shaped recovery," \uc2 CIMB\uc2 -GK economist Song Seng Wun said.\par \par \uc2 "There may be occasional sprouts of green shoots, but...I think the numbers we're seeing is a reminder that it's dangerous to extrapolate," Song said.\par \par \uc2 For the full year, Singapore's government now expects non-oil domestic exports to fall between 10% and 13%; the government earlier forecast exports would contract between 9% and 11%.\par \par \uc2 Seasonally adjusted and on a month-by-month basis, exports fell 1.3% in April, compared with a 10.4% rise in March; the surveyed economists had forecast a 0.5% drop in April.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar barely budged on the export data, largely because the decline was close to expectations. The local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  last traded at 1.4690 at 0719 GMT.\par \par \uc2 In a related development, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  has \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets at least three times this month, buying U.S. dollars to smooth the Singapore dollar's rise, according to a person familiar with the situation.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's export competitiveness is highly dependent on the strength of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  relative to major partner's \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , especially the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Singapore is "happy" with the local dollar's current level but the MAS will buy the U.S. dollar again if it falls convincingly below S$1.4600, the person said.\par \par \uc2 -By P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; +65 64154 152; venkat.pr@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  (Se Young Lee, Costas Paris contributed to this article) \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 18-05-09 0728GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090518e55i0009y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
