\par\par \b \uc2 DJ CORRECT:Singapore Warns On Sing Dlr Rise; No Policy Shift\b0\par\par\uc2 822 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 Mai 2009\par 03:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 ("=2nd UPDATE:Singapore Warns On Sing Dlr Rise; No Policy Shift," published at 0841 GMT Thursday, incorrectly stated OCBC's forecast for Singapore's 2009 GDP in the third-to-last paragraph. The error also appeared in "=UPDATE: \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  Threatens \b \highlight22\uc2 FX Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; No Policy Shift," published at 0558 GMT. The correct version follows.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Costas Paris and P.R. Venkat \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2  (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to brake the local dollar's rise, a person familiar with the situation said Thursday, while the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  reaffirmed there is no change in its policy for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  will buy the U.S. dollar "if it falls below S$1.4700, around S$1.4690," the person familiar with the situation told Dow Jones Newswires. He said S$1.4700 to the U.S. dollar roughly equates with the strong end of the Singapore dollar in terms of the undisclosed trade-weighted band that the MAS uses to guide the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 These remarks were "a very strong signal" to the market that the MAS doesn't want the Singapore dollar pushing too high, said a trader with a foreign bank. But he predicted the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to rise in coming days if there are no negative economic surprises.\par \par \uc2 And indeed, a top \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  official separately reiterated the MAS doesn't plan to deliberately drive the Singapore dollar lower over time.\par \par \uc2 "We keep our monetary policy on the medium-term inflation outlook and taking into account growth prospects," Managing Director Heng Swee Keat told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview. "We don't use the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for competitiveness because it is not sustainable to align \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  just for competitiveness."\par \par \uc2 Heng declined to comment on specific levels for the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 The MAS uses the exchange rate, rather than interest rates, as its chief policy tool, because trade flows dwarf the island's small domestic economy.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last month eased policy by lowering the midpoint of the Singapore dollar band. But it kept the slope of the trading band flat, surprising some market players who had expected the MAS to drive the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  lower, via a downward slope for the policy band, in order to help exporters.\par \par \uc2 The MAS has been rumored to be \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  quietly in the market in recent days, traders say. The Singapore dollar has joined other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets in climbing on improved risk appetite as the global recession shows signs of slowing and markets take heart at reported results of U.S. "stress tests" of the nation's major banks.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar, which had traded as high as about S$1.58 in early March, has slid steadily in the past two months as global sentiment has rebounded. It dipped Thursday to four-month low of S$1.4691 - just at the levels signaled as the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  zone - before rising modestly. The greenback was quoted at S$1.4718 around 0840 GMT.\par \par \uc2 In addition to its importance for the MAS trading band, the S$1.4700 level is represents a key technical level for the U.S. dollar.\par \par \uc2 Daily charts show strong support in the S$1.4620-S$1.4700 area, but a weekly close below that zone could target a rapid decline to the U.S. dollar's December trough of $1.4150, said Dow Jones CommentaryPlus analyst Mark Cranfield. The greenback closed Wednesday below its 200-day moving average at S$1.4804, adding to bearish signals as it is already trading below its 30-, 55-, and 100-day averages.\par \par \uc2 "The U.S. dollar is weakening in response to the risk rally," said \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Kit Wei Zheng. Singapore "is also attracting capital inflows on the equity side, so that's pushing the Singapore dollar stronger."\par \par \uc2 But while the mood among investors has brightened, export-dependent Singapore remains mired in its worst recession since independence in 1965.\par \par \uc2 "Fundamentally as far as the Singapore economy, nothing has changed," said OCBC strategist Selena Ling, who forecasts the city-state's economy to contract 7.6% this year - within the government forecast range of between 6% nd 9% contraction. "It's just all sentiment. It's just that people had expected the worst, so going from here they're looking for the upside - or they want to believe in the upside."\par \par \uc2 The MAS's Heng said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will stick to its policy.\par \par \uc2 "Our assessment is that the current monetary policy stance remains appropriate," he said. "There are no new developments that cause us to change our stance."\par \par \uc2 -By Costas Paris, P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4151; costas.paris@dowjones.com \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    (Se Young Lee contributed to this item.) \par  \f2