\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ FOCUS: Singapore  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\uc2  Likely To Ease Policy In April\b0\par\par\uc2 By Se Young Lee  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 955 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 07:29\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will likely ease monetary policy in April, weakening the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to stem the damage to the small trade-dependent nation from the global downturn.\par \par \uc2 But given doubts that driving down the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  sharply would rescue the recessionary economy from the collapse in external demand, the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore is unlikely to shift to a policy of sustained \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  depreciation, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The city-state's outlook has grown increasingly bleak.\par \par \uc2 Gross domestic product shrank at an annual rate of 16.4% in the last three months of 2008 - the biggest contraction since the government began releasing seasonally adjusted data in 1976. Exports and manufacturing output plunged at record rates in January. Minister Mentor Lee Kuan Yew said GDP could contract by much as 10% this year, which would be by far be the worst fall on record.\par \par \uc2 The dire signals are putting heavy pressure on the authorities to take additional steps to limit downside risks. The government has ramped up fiscal policy and now the heat is on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The MAS uses the Singapore dollar exchange rate, rather than interest rates, as its chief policy tool because trade dwarfs the island's small domestic economy. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which manages the Singapore dollar in an undisclosed trade-weighted band, is to hold its next half-yearly policy review in April, although a date hasn't been set.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  economist Kit Wei Zheng said the MAS has been "consistently \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar" to keep it within the bottom quartile of the policy band.\par \par \uc2 "If our model is correct, this suggests that MAS has already engineered a de facto easing of policy within the parameters of the current 'neutral' policy stance," he said. "But this de facto easing has already reached its natural limits, and, therefore, the next step would be to be to engineer an explicit easing of policy."\par \par \uc2 Assuming an April easing, \uc2 Westpac\uc2  forecasts the U.S. dollar will rise to S$1.61 by the end of June. Standard Chartered expects the greenback to hit S$1.57 by midyear but end the year lower at S$1.48.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , quoted at S$1.5268 around 0630 GMT Wednesday, has risen 6.3% so far this year against the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and is far above the all-time low of S$1.3438 it hit July 15.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  last eased policy at October's policy review by shifting to a neutral stance, ending a policy of guiding the Singapore dollar gradually higher to fight previously rising inflation.\par \par \uc2 Kit is among many analysts who say the MAS will ease policy in April, likely through a downward re-centering of the Singapore dollar's trading band - a move that would immediately weaken the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - rather than a more aggressive shift to a downward sloping band, which would aim to steadily weaken the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 MAS Deputy Managing Director Ong Chong Tee in January suggested the authorities were reluctant to commit to guiding the Singapore dollar lower over a sustained period. "The current slowdown reflects a sharp slowdown in global demand and not on (Singapore's) competitiveness," he said, adding there was "no reason, in our view, for a persistent weakening of the Singapore dollar."\par \par \uc2 Singapore's non-oil domestic exports contracted for a 10th straight month in February, falling 23.7% from a year earlier. While that wasn't as bad as January's record 34.9% drop, shipments to almost all major trading partners, such as the U.S. and the European Union, remained weak.\par \par \uc2 Driving down Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could also lead to other headaches, such as further downward pressure on asset prices that could trigger fund outflows.\par \par \uc2 "Weakening the Singapore dollar to gain a competitive edge in trade could also backfire and lead to retaliatory actions by regional economies and major trading partners," said Standard Chartered economist Alvin Liew in a report. "In such a competition no one wins, but everyone pays dearly."\par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, a move by MAS to aid the economy is likely.\par \par \uc2 "There do not seem to be solid grounds to believe that fiscal policies will carry the torch for the rest of the way," market research firm 4CAST said in a report. "Complementary monetary policy easing will be imperative in creating economic conditions more conducive for a recovery when conditions begin to pick up once again. "\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2 's Kit said the October easing stopped but didn't reverse a tightening of monetary conditions that occurred when the MAS re-centered the Singapore dollar's trading band upward in April to curb inflation. He said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's real effective exchange rate (REER) in fact appreciated in January and has strengthened by 10%-20% against most Asian counterparts since end of 2007.\par \par \uc2 "The Singapore dollar REER appreciation, alongside the rise in real interest rates (due to moderating inflation) has resulted in a tightening of overall monetary conditions since January at a time when it is least needed," he said.\par \par \uc2 Kit said a sufficiently aggressive move from the MAS will "do the trick," in which case it will simply need to maintain the status quo for "another two policy meetings or so."\par \par \uc2 "Maybe in the second half of 2010 when you have firmer sign of a more decisive pickup, then maybe they will shift towards a more appreciation stance again," he said.\par \par \uc2 -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4155; vincent.lee@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 March 18, 2009 02:29 ET (06:29 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020090821e53i0nvmt\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
