\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Higher Late Ahead Of US Jobs Data; Bonds Up\b0\par\par\uc2 265 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 M\u228\'00\'E4rz 2009\par 08:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2009 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD              1.5475   -0.0048 \par    Overnight rate        0.19%    Unchanged \par    Two-year bond yield:  0.59%   -3 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   1.98%   -3 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  1.78%   +2 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   2.48%   -3 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   70 bps  -5 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was higher in late Asian trade Friday, as the greenback slid against the majors and other regional \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar retreated from a high of S$1.5561 reached early in the session, trading as low as S$1.5474. But the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  settled into a relatively narrow range for most of the day as market players await U.S. February non-farm payrolls data due later in the global day.\par \par \uc2 Analysts say a poor showing from the U.S. jobs data could lead to renewed risk aversion, which would push the U.S. dollar higher. And with a grim outlook for the local economy, the market remains biased against the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 But recent talk of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's fall may force the market to act with restraint in the near term.\par \par \uc2 Local government bonds rose, tracking gains in U.S. Treasurys overnight as local equities remained weak due to a lack of market visibility in the near term.\par \par \uc2 -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4155; vincent.lee@dowjones.com [ 06-03-09 0724GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020090306e53600096\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
