\par\par \b \uc2 DJ MARKET TALK: Aussie Govt Credit  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Inevitable - DB\b0\par\par\uc2 1,572 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Januar 2009\par 02:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2009, Dow Jones & Company, Inc. \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0102 GMT [Dow Jones] Renewed Australian government \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  into credit markets inevitable, and would be best served through buying mortgage-backed securities or semi government bonds, also an insurance scheme to guarantee new loans, says \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  Strategist \uc2 David Plank\uc2 ; "further cannibalization of the Government's AAA-rating is the least preferred option." Says by not extending guarantee to new forms of debt, semi government bonds and bank bonds won't be crowded out; also problematic is 'Commons' approach, whereby actions of a single issuer using guarantee impacts pricing for all AAA-rated issuers. "The levels at which it may make sense for a single guaranteed MBS bond to issue could well have negative implications for every other AAA-rated issuer." Says to widen coverage of guarantee, government would need to step up its regulation; "if the Government feels more \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is necessary then our preference would be direct purchase of assets and an insurance scheme for banks."(EGC)\par \par \uc2 0056 GMT [Dow Jones] Korean banks down on concerns "that not enough companies under evaluation are going to be liquidated, which will, in effect, hurt banks' soundness in the long run," says \uc2 Kiwoom Securities\uc2  ' Seo Young-soo. Note, creditor banks reviewing 92 construction, 19 shipbuilding companies since early this month to see whether they are viable businesses, results likely end week. Bank shares also down on news of likely big losses at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  for 2008. KB Financial (105560.SE) down 4.9% at KRW33,850, Shinhan Financial (055550.SE) down 3.9% at KRW28,450, \uc2 Hana Financial\uc2  (086790.SE) down 5.4% at KRW20,150. "Today's drop seems rather overly done, so there may be some recovery later on."(JYY)\par \par \uc2 0055 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY may rise in OTC market given gains in USD offshore overnight; central parity likely set around 6.8390 vs 6.8360 yesterday, says Shanghai-based foreign bank trader. "But the central parity has limited upside as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  doesn't want the yuan to have a clear depreciating trend." Pair closed at 6.8359.(WYL)\par \par \uc2 0047 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI likely lower on concerns over health of global banking sector following news \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  expects to post large loss for FY08; pullback in other Asian markets so far sets negative tone for session, with dealers tipping KLCI to test and possibly breach 879 (30 day moving average). Ended Monday at 890.28, down 0.7%. Resistance put at 900. "Banking stocks and blue chips such as Tenaga (5347.KU), TM (4863.KU), TMI (6888.KU) and MISC (3816.KU) are likely to face some pressure," says one. OSK expects range-bound session for KLCI before \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  meeting tomorrow, where economists look for rate cut of at least 25 bps.(VGB)\par \par \uc2 0044 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD likely to continue rising vs 6-week high last close at 33.535 on continued USD strength vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight, widening premium in NDFs, says foreign bank trader, tipping resistance at 33.700. "Taiwan's economy is still not doing well fundamentally, evident in economic data such as exports. Foreign investors have also been more active buyers of the U.S. dollar as they are bearish toward the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ." Still, exporters tipped to cap pair's gains, with trader eyeing support at 33.500. "It's a seasonal thing. They need to sell U.S. dollars for local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the Lunar New Year holiday" (Jan. 26-Jan. 30). \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  market open until 0930GMT this week vs 0800GMT normally due to week-long Lunar New Year holiday next week. (OKH)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/SGD last bid 1.5007 vs 1.4970 morning trade, 1.4890 late Asia trade yesterday as risk aversion returns on gloomy outlook for global economy, says trader with local bank. "With a string of bad news about the economy, especially from the euro zone, the USD is defacto strengthening," says Emmanuel Ng of \uc2 OCBC\uc2 ; also points to expectations of a lower 2009 GDP growth forecast by \uc2 Singapore government\uc2  tomorrow. Tips resistance at 1.5030.(PRV)\par \par \uc2 0040 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi down 2.3% at 1124.24 in light volume, reflecting losses in European markets, driven by fundamental concerns, says Lee Woo-hyun at KTB Securities; "Today's fall is showing how fragile sentiment is amid ongoing concerns about poor fundamental and corporate earnings." Expects Kospi to test 1100 support again; also, thin markets expected in run up to Lunar new year holidays, which could make for more volatility. \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  (005930.SE) down 2.7% at KRW462,000, \uc2 LG\uc2  Display (034220.SE) down 3.3% at KRW25,150. Banks falling on ongoing concerns about their financial health with KB Financial (105560.SE) down 4.9% at KRW33,850. But KT (030200.SE) +2.1% at KRW40,600, KT Freetel (032390.SE) +2.6% at KRW29,200 on Korean Economic Daily report that board of KT will decide on merger with its mobile service unit, KT Freetel, later today. (SKS)\par \par \uc2 0036 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 2.5% at 8052.98, at intraday low, as many investors taking cautious stance ahead of earnings reports from Japanese firms, says Yumi Nishimura, market analyst at Daiwa Securities SMBC; adds weakness in EUR/JPY (now in high 117s down over 3.0 ticks from prior day pre-market level) also weighing. "After earnings forecast cuts at many Japanese firms, investors still fear more negative earnings-related cues." Expects support at 8000, with resistance around yesterday's intraday low of 8221.84. Support at 8000 due to hopes that President-elect Barack Obama upon inauguration may make uplifting comments related to his economic policies, she says. Range for session 8052.98 - 8190.42; 32 of 33 Topix subsectors down. Shares of major banks lower, with \uc2 Mizuho Financial Group (8411.TO) \uc2 down 4.1% at Y233 and \uc2 Sumitomo Mitsui Financial Group\uc2  (8316.TO) down 3.3% at Y3,550. Tech stocks also weak, with \uc2 Sony\uc2  (6758.TO) down 2.9% at Y2,020 and Advantest (6857.TO) down 5.8% at Y1,229. (JUO)\par \par \uc2 0035 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares likely to continue consolidating in 4300-4400 range after ending flat at 4366.76 yesterday, ahead of Lunar New Year holidays, says KGI Securities' Randy Chang; "investors are not willing to keep positions during the long break, so there won't be much buying these few days." Stock market shut from Thursday. Expects solar energy companies to rise after Economic Daily News reports solar energy companies such as Green Energy Technology (3519.TW), Gintech Energy (3514.TW) will continue operating during Lunar New Year holidays to finish orders. News of expected massive losses at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  , jitters over UK banking system, may weigh on financial shares today (sector ends +0.1% yesterday).(WYI)\par \par \uc2 0033 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW up at 1,374.9 vs yesterday's 1,362.5 close on early Kospi weakness. Also, concerns about UK banking system, ratings downgrade for Spain damping players' risk appetite, providing support for pair, says Woori Futures analyst Shin Jin-ho; expects pair to meet resistance at 1,380, says Kospi likely key market focus. Local bank trader notes there're still lots of bids for USD, participants inclined to snatch up USD whenever it retreats a bit. Another trader says importers, exporters movements ahead of Lunar New Year holiday also likely to be key.(MJL)\par \par \uc2 0023 GMT [Dow Jones] JGBs open down but claw their way back on weakness in Tokyo shares, leaving market mixed, \uc2 Mitsubishi UFJ Securities\uc2  strategist Naomi Hasegawa says; lead March futures down just 0.03 at 139.51, while yield on 10- and 20-year cash bonds +1.5 bps at 1.26% and 1.845% respectively. Big event of day is MOF auction of Y600 billion in 30-year notes; "The auction should go smoothly. The 30-years are relatively cheap compared to the rest of curve, and the \uc2 BOJ\uc2  is going to expand JGB buying to include 30-year bonds. So I think the market will digest this auction," Hasegawa says. Tips futures to stay in 139.15-139.65 range today, 10-year cash bond yield in 1.235%-1.265% band. (MSA)\par \par \uc2 0022 GMT [Dow Jones] CORRECTION: S&P/ASX 200 opens well below expectations, falling 2.6% to 3495.3 in early trade, on bad headlines overnight from U.K about \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2  , which flagged huge annual loss. "Investors often get spooked by these sorts of headlines and it's put a lot of pressure on our banks today," says Shaw Stockbroking trader Todd Kerslake. Adds, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) and \uc2 BHP Billiton\uc2  (BHP.AU) also down following falls in London as investors continue to fret about waning demand for commodities. Adds, bad numbers from \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  Friday also hurting local sentiment. "Lets hope that this is a clearing of the decks and they can get back to business, use TARP money, do what they have to do and get back to some sort of normality." Says that Obama inauguration might spur a relief rally on Wall St. (An item at 0000 GMT misstated the date relating to \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  .) (RRK)\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow 