\par\par \b \uc2 3rd UPDATE: Asian Shrs In North/South Divide; Japan, HK Jump\b0\par\par\uc2 1,145 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2008\par 07:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds quotes, information, updates/adds market levels)\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets were a little mixed Tuesday with those to the north making a large intraday recovery but some southeast Asian bourses suffering as the exodus from smaller emerging markets continued.\par \par \uc2 Even with the gains in Japan, Korea, Taiwan and Hong Kong, sentiment was cautious; trading ranges remained very wide and markets swung often from negative to positive and back again.\par \par \uc2 "Given the 'trading mentality' within Asia, the key question remains how long this change in sentiment will last," said IG Markets dealing head Oliver Stevens.\par \par \uc2 The contrast was marked: Korea's Kospi Composite shot up 5.6%, aided by buying of large-cap stocks by the nation's pension fund, with Japan's Nikkei 225 ending up 6.4% and the Hong Kong Hang Seng Index rallying 10.2%; but Indonesia's main index was recently down 4.8% and Singapore's Straits Times Index down 4%.\par \par \uc2 "The concern is the whole emerging markets space is in big trouble over the next 12 months," said First NZ Capital Strategist Jason Wong.\par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen had turned sharply lower and U.S. stock futures were now higher, with S&P 500 futures quoted up 2% and Dow futures up 138 points, though European spreadbetters were calling stocks there to open mildly lower.\par \par \uc2 There was much event-risk on the slate the rest of the week, including a Federal Reserve meeting on rates, more earnings from key corporates and an estimate on third quarter U.S. gross domestic product.\par \par \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  analysts said the GDP data would be poor, and could be a catalyst for further declines: "Bad news for the U.S. to be sure, but worse news for many other countries whose growth was largely a function of exports to the U.S.," they said.\par \par \uc2 While some markets were being propped up by pension funds at the behest of governments, a senior trader at a major brokerage in Australia said capitulation selling was picking up; "retail investors, pension funds, insurers, sovereigns and corporates are having more impact in this leg down than the hedge fund community. It's like a global margin call for non-leveraged investors."\par \par \uc2 Macquarie Equities didn't expect global equity markets to bottom until well into 2009, with a wave of earnings downgrades yet to come; "market volatility will continue as short, sharp rallies act as false signals," it said.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei 225 made an intraday recovery from a low of 6994.90; the finance minister said the government will immediately tighten regulations on so-called naked short-selling of stocks, about a week ahead of the original schedule.\par \par \uc2 Fast Retailing\uc2  gained 7% after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  lifted that stock to a buy rating, while Fanuc gained 9.4%; the maker of factory automation systems and robots cut its full-year forecasts but not by as much as investors had feared.\par \par \uc2 Australia's S&P/ASX 200 ended down 0.4%, though off a four-year low of 3724.8. Miners found support with BHP Billiton up 3.1% and \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  up 5.6%; financials were weak with \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  falling 4.3%, \uc2 Suncorp-Metway\uc2  down 5.7% and AMP off 5.1%.\par \par \uc2 The Kospi was aided by domestic institutions, which were net buyers of shares worth KRW320.5 billion, with pension funds purchasing shares worth KRW161.3 billion; among shipbuilders and steelmakers, \uc2 Hyundai Heavy Industries\uc2  jumped the daily limit of 15% and \uc2 Posco\uc2  soared 13.7%.\par \par \uc2 Bellwether Samsung Electronics rose 5.8%; \uc2 UBS\uc2  raised its rating on the stock to neutral from a sell after a 50% fall in its stock price since May this year.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan shares ended 0.8% higher after an initial fall of more than 5%; government funds bought "a few billion" Taiwan dollars' worth of shares, traders said, and also bought stock futures.\par \par \uc2 Hong Kong's HSI was taking back its big fall on Monday, with large support from \uc2 HSBC\uc2 , up 7.3% after a 5-day, 30.0% loss.\par \par \uc2 The STI was in negative territory after a holiday Monday; "there's no sign of bargain hunting. It's all one way," said a trader at a local brokerage. Banks were weak with DBS down 9.2%.\par \par \uc2 Malaysia's KLCI was down 6%, also after a holiday on Monday. Indonesian shares were lower by 4.8% though there was some support from local funds; Philippine shares ended 0.6% lower after a 12% fall on Monday while Vietnamese shares ended down 2%.\par \par \uc2 Thai shares were the sole standout in Southeast Asia, gaining 2.8% after a large fall Monday.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand shares fell 3.3% to their lowest level since July 2004, after Monday's holiday there; Fletcher Building, which is exposed to the housing sector, fell 4.7% to a four-year low.\par \par \uc2 In \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, the yen gave back its early gains. Standard Chartered Bank was tipping imminent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to sell the yen though there was no sign the day's action involved the Bank of Japan; rather, analysts cited the gain in the Nikkei: "Players are watching only stock price movements. I can't find any other factors," said a senior sales dealer at a major Japan bank.\par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar was around Y95.40 from Y92.66 earlier, with the euro at Y119.58 from Y114.43; the euro was around US$1.2532. The Australian dollar was also off its lows, at US$0.6162, with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to buy the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  earlier by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  there.\par \par \uc2 Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were under pressure still; the Korean won ended at its lowest level since April 1998, with official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  spotted to sell the dollar in Malaysia and \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bonds were somewhat quiet with lead futures down 0.13 at 137.99.\par \par \uc2 Base metals came off their session lows with the euro on the rise; LME three-month copper was still down $21 at $3,999 a ton but off a low of $3,825. Spot gold was at $730.30 an ounce, up $1.70.\par \par \uc2 Front-month Nymex crude reversed early falls, even as concerns remained about the demand outlook; it was up 59 cents at $63.81 per barrel on Globex.\par \par \uc2 -By Rosalind Mathieson and Kathryn O'Keeffe, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4140; Rosalind.Mathieson@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 28-10-08 0633GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020081028e4as00093\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
