\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore's money rates fall on c.bank fund injection\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 September 2008\par 11:40\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Sept 30 (Reuters) - Singapore's money market rates fell on Tuesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  injected funds to ease credit strains caused by global financial turmoil, traders said.\par \par \uc2  One-month interbank market rates (SIBOR) <SISGDD=ABSG> fell to 1.85 percent on Tuesday from 2.06 percent on Monday and retreated from a nine-month high at 2.275 percent hit on Friday.\par \par \uc2  Singapore's overnight rates <SGDF=> fell to 0.875 percent on Tuesday from 2.125 percent on Monday, traders said.\par \par \uc2  Traders said the falling money market rates indicated the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) had injected money.\par \par \uc2  "No doubt the MAS will be there to stabilise the money market conditions, but they are dealing in a global environment that has turned irrational," said Claudio Piron, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JPMorgan Chase in Singapore.\par \par \uc2  "There are signs around the region that counter-party risk and risk aversion is starting to push rates high as banks limit their credit," he said.\par \par \uc2  The MAS may have sold U.S. dollars in recent weeks to support the weakening Singapore dollar, which required it to add  liquidity into the local market to help offset the effect of such \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's interbank rates had long been held down by steady inflows of foreign money, which put pressure on the Singapore dollar to rise and in turn force the MAS to buy U.S. dollars to curb its strength.\par \par \uc2  It then released money into the local money market, keeping rates low as it failed to sterilise the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts say.\par \par \uc2  But the picture has changed in recent weeks as the global credit turbulence has prodded foreigners to dump Singapore's stocks <.FTSTI>, which have fallen by a quarter since early June.\par \par \uc2  "Outflows and concerns over counterparty risks naturally put some upside pressure on the U.S. dollar against the Singapore dollar," said Han Sia Yeo, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America.\par \par \uc2  Reflecting the trend of capital outflows and weakening exports, Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves fell by $4.86 billion in August to $170.1 billion.\par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> fell to 1.4345 per U.S. dollar on Tuesday, down some six percent from its record high of 1.3453 hit on July 15 amid a region-wide squeeze on the U.S. dollar funding.\par \par \uc2  Most analysts expect the MAS, which steers monetary policy by managing its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within an undisclosed trading band rather than adjusting interest rates, is likely to slow the pace of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains when it reviews policy on October 10.\par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  shifted the centre of that band upwards in April to try to quell inflation, which has started easing.\par \par \uc2  Mark Tan and Michael Buchanan, analysts at Goldman Sachs, said in a note that they expected the Singapore dollar to fall to 1.45 and 1.47 per U.S. dollar over the next six and 12 months.\par \par \uc2  Singapore's data issued last week showed the country's August factory output fell a seasonally adjusted 1.9 percent from July and dropped 12.2 percent from the same month in 2007.  (Reporting by Kevin Yao; Editing by Clarence Fernandez)  \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-SINGAPORE/RATES|LANGEN|ABN|M|RBN|