\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia mixed; won sinks despite  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 August 2008\par 05:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 SINGAPORE\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Aug 22 (Reuters) - The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar and Chinese yuan rose against a broadly weaker U.S. dollar on Friday, but the beleaguered South Korean won hit its weakest level in nearly four years despite official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar <SGD=> rose as far as 1.4026 per U.S. dollar, up almost 0.7 percent from late Asian trade on Thursday to its strongest level since August 13.\par \par \uc2 "The Singapore dollar is following general dollar movement," said a trader in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "Some longs from yesterday from above 1.4100 cut all the way down in New York market to 1.4026 and local banks tried to push up to sell (U.S.) dollars this morning," the trader added.\par \par \uc2 The Malaysian ringgit <MYR=> eased back to 3.3410 per dollar, virtually flat from late Asian trade on Thursday, after briefly rising to 3.3260 in early trade.\par \par \uc2 "The dollar has not fallen as much against the ringgit despite its drop against majors," said a trader in Kuala Lumpur.\par \par \uc2 Analysts polled by Reuters expected Malaysia's July inflation, due to be released later in the day, to hit a 27-\u189\'00\'BD high due to higher fuel and electricity costs, but they see only a small chance of an interest rate rise next week. [ID:nKLR328694]\par \par \uc2 The dollar index, a gauge of its performance against a basket of six major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , edged up to 76.30 <.DXY> after falling 1.2 percent on Thursday -- the biggest one-day drop since March when the index slumped to an all-time low.\par \par \uc2 A surge in commodity prices and worries about the U.S. financial sector are taking the wind out of the dollar's recent rally which was fuelled by unwinding of positions in the euro, gold and oil.\par \par \uc2 Investors awaited comments from Fed Chairman Ben Bernanke, who will speak on financial stability later in the day. [ID:nN21283859]\par \par \uc2 BELEAGUERED WON\par \par \uc2 The Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> gained a quarter of a percent to a fresh three-week high at 6.8271 per dollar after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  set the daily mid-point rate sharply higher.\par \par \uc2 The yuan's rise has stalled in recent weeks, but many analysts expect the pace of its appreciation to pick up steadily as the country still needs a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in inflation.\par \par \uc2 The yuan has emerged as Asia's best performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , gaining 6.9 percent so far this year, as its regional peers lost ground amid credit market woes and inflation fears.\par \par \uc2 The won <KRW=> fell as far as 1,062.6 per dollar, its weakest level since December 2004, even as traders cited dollar sales by the authorities to prop up the ailing unit.\par \par \uc2 The won, the worst performing \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in Asia, has lost almost 12 percent against the dollar so far this year amid concerns about high oil prices and an economic downturn.\par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> briefly hit 31.308 per U.S. dollar, up a quarter of a percent from Thursday's close.\par \par \uc2 Analysts at DBS Bank said they expected the Taiwan dollar to meet resistance at 31 as the island's economic growth slows and the U.S. dollar strengthens.\par \par \uc2 "Having broken above its 5-month trading range between 30 and 31, we believe that the bias remain to the upside for U.S. dollar/Taiwan dollar as long as slowing growth threatens to take the stock market lower," they said in a note.\par \par \uc2 Taiwan's GDP data is due later in the day [ID:nTP322786] \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. DOLLAR Change on the day at 0753 GMT \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       109.08         108.86       -0.20\par  Sing dlr        1.4074         1.4117       +0.31\par  Taiwan dlr      31.365         31.389       +0.08\par  Korean won     1061.90        1053.50       -0.79\par  Baht             33.95          33.89       -0.18\par  Peso             45.66          45.46       -0.44\par  Rupiah         9135.00        9147.00       +0.13\par  Rupee            43.40          43.51       +0.25\par  Ringgit         3.3410         3.3400       -0.03\par  Yuan            6.8310         6.8442       +0.19\par  Change so far in 2008\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       109.08         111.33       +2.06\par  Sing dlr        1.4074         1.4382       +2.19\par  Taiwan dlr      31.365         32.443       +3.44\par  Korean won     1061.90         935.70      -11.88\par  Baht             33.95          33.68       -0.80\par  Peso             45.66          41.28       -9.59\par  Rupiah         9135.00        9390.00       +2.79\par  Rupee            43.40          39.41       -9.19\par  Ringgit         3.3410         3.3050       -1.08\par  Yuan            6.8310         7.3041       +6.93\par \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Editing by Tomasz Janowski)\f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  (UPDATE 1)|LANGEN|Table|ABN|M|D|E|RBN|IF|PHD|SI|Z|J|SNS|SNI|RNP|DNP|PTD   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080825e48m000e4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
