\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore Dlr Lower Late; Hits 4-Mo Low As Econ Worries Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 546 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 8 August 2008\par 09:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD              1.3978   +0.0158 \par    Overnight rate        0.63%   +19 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  1.07%   -2 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   3.16%   -5 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  2.27%   -4 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   3.46%   -16 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   119 bps -12 bps \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was lower in Asian trade late Friday, hitting a new four-month low in early trade on the back of broad greenback strength and concerns about the island state's growth prospects.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar hit an intraday high of S$1.4010, almost two Singapore cents higher than the levels in Asian trade late Thursday. The greenback was supported by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Jean-Claude Trichet's comments overnight that the Eurozone's economic growth later this year will likely be "particularly weak" as inflationary pressures hurt domestic and global demand.\par \par \uc2 Market watchers also said the bleak outlook for Singapore's economy is also weighing on the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With talks of a possible downward revision from Singapore's 2008 economic growth forecast of 4%-6%, investors' sentiment is souring on the Singapore dollar.\par \par \uc2 "There's some talk that the government will narrow the forecast band or adjust it lower to 3%-5% or 3.5%-5.5%," said a strategist with a local bank.\par \par \uc2 "We're seeing more signals like Finance Minister Tharman (Shanmugaratnam)'s comment Thursday that Singapore's economic growth will be weak, so that's some kind of a foreshadowing."\par \par \uc2 While some traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  may have \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in small ways to slow the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar's fall in recent sessions, the strategist said the MAS will likely still allow the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slide in an effort to boost the island state's ailing exports.\par \par \uc2 "Any expectations for further tightening by the MAS in October have evaporated, and the momentum may be for the (trade-weighted) Singapore dollar to keep falling," OCBC strategist Emmanuel Ng said. "That said, there's a chance the MAS will come into the market to keep volatility under control."\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  revised upward its U.S. dollar-Singapore dollar forecast to S$1.42 by the year's end from S$1.36, noting a "decisive shift in the balance of risks toward growth."\par \par \uc2 The bank's economist Kit Wei Zheng said in a report that the MAS' decision to allow the local unit to fall despite that it has sufficient foreign reserves to prop the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can be seen as a "de-facto" easing without a policy change.\par \par \uc2 Traders said the Singapore dollar will likely continue to slide in the current environment, where the market's focus has shifted away from U.S. economic woes to dimming growth prospects elsewhere.\par \par \uc2 Local government bonds were higher, with more interest in the long-term paper, as the focus shifted away from inflationary pressures to growth prospects. A trader with a foreign bank said investors are likely seeking a safe haven away from the depressed stock market.\par \par \uc2 -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4155; vincent.lee@dowjones.com [ 08-08-08 0755GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1163 1859   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080808e488000z2\par }\page 