\par\par \b \uc2 Singapore cbank dollar buying to depress money rates\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kevin Yao   \par \uc2 743 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Juli 2008\par 06:17\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, July 15 (Reuters) - Singapore's depressed money market rates will stay that way for a while as the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  continues to buy dollars to limit \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation, flooding the market with money, analysts said on Tuesday.\par \par \uc2 Three-month interbank market rates (SIBOR) <SISGDD=ABSG> fell to 1.157 percent on Monday from 1.163 percent on Friday, staying close to a three-year low of 1.156 percent hit last week.\par \par \uc2 Six-month interbank rates fell to 1.50 percent on Monday from from 1.813 percent in early June.\par \par \uc2 Singapore's interbank rates have been falling steadily from the middle of 2006, when the three-month rate peaked at about 3.6 percent, coinciding with steep gains in the local dollar since then.\par \par \uc2 Analysts expect the short-term rates to stay weak in the coming months as foreign money flows in, putting pressure on the Singapore dollar to rise and forcing the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore (MAS) to buy U.S. dollars to curb its strength.\par \par \uc2 "The easier money rates have been an 'anomaly' that are probably partly attributable to continued inflows that are keeping interbank liquidity flush," said Emmanuel Ng, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 OCBC Bank\uc2  in Singapore.\par \par \uc2 "There is little impetus (for the rates) to rise significantly in the near term unless we see a generalised withdrawal of investor flows out of Asia en masse," he added.\par \par \uc2 But Chia Woon Khien, an analyst at \uc2 Royal Bank of Scotland\uc2 , said she believed that Singapore's money rates would bottom out later this year as weakening exports reduce the amount of U.S. dollar inflows and ease upward pressures on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "Considering that liquidity conditions are still flush, money market rates have fallen quite a lot lately, but I'm not so sure if rates have more room to fall," she said.\par \par \uc2 The MAS, which is torn between the dilemma of containing inflation running at 26-year highs while trying to avoid a sharp economic slowdown, is expected to stick to its tight policy at its review in October but refrain from tightening further.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which conducts monetary policy by managing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a secret trading band against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  instead of setting interest rates, shifted the centre of that band upwards in April to try to quell inflation.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCY\b0 \highlight\uc2  RISES\par \par \uc2 Analysts say this unique policy constrains the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  because it has to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to control the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and in the process it has no sway over rates in the money market.\par \par \uc2 Han Sia Yeo, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Bank of America\uc2 , said he expected the Singapore dollar to stay close to the estimated upper end of the band in the near term, but gradually drift lower towards its mid-point later this year as inflation risks begin to recede.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=>, viewed by investors as a safe bet in Asia, gained 0.3 percent on Tuesday to 1.3504 per U.S. dollar, its highest level since April 23.\par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar has gained 6.6 percent this year despite frequent dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the authorities, trailing behind the Chinese yuan <CNY=CFXS> and Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP>.\par \par \uc2 By contrast, fears of inflation and credit woes have hit most Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, particularly the South Korean won <KRW=>, the Indian rupee <INR=IN> and Philippine peso <PHP=>, prompting dollar-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop up the units.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's bond yield curve <0#SGBMK=> is flattening with yields on two-year bonds rising 25 basis points in the past week and the spread between 2- and 20-year bonds narrowing to 270 basis points from 305 basis points a week ago.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the flattening yield curve was partially influenced by recent data which showed that the economy shrank by an annualised and seasonally adjusted 6.6 percent in the second quarter -- the sharpest contraction in five years.\par \par \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  has cut its forecast for Singapore's 2008 economic growth to 4.1 percent from 4.7 percent, citing slowing exports amid the U.S. slowdown, analyst Kit Wei Zheng said in a note.\par \par \uc2 "Given the growth slowdown, moderating inflation and lagged effects of past policy tightening, we expect the MAS to maintain its current policy stance in October," he said. (Editing by Jacqueline Wong)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-SINGAPORE-RATES|LANGEN|ABN|M|E|RBN|D|RNP   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020080715e47f000at\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
