\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Yuan leads ringgit, Singapore dollar to highs   \b0\par\par\uc2 706 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 25 Januar 2008\par 04:31\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Jan 25 (Reuters) - Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  extended gains for a third day on Friday, riding on improving risk appetite after an aggressive U.S. rate cut and a jump in the Chinese yuan, with the Singapore dollar hitting its highest level in a decade.   \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar and Malaysian ringgit hit 10-year highs, and the Philippine peso saw the biggest gains in the region on while the yuan <CNY=CFXS> climbed to 7.2070 per dollar, its highest levels since its July 2005 revaluation.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts said the yuan was boosted by a report indicating Chinese growth will remain strong in 2008, notwithstanding the risks of a U.S. recession, and another report suggesting China will allow a faster pace of appreciation of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . [nPEK85240] [nPEK96371]   \par \par \uc2  "Comments from China do seem to be confirming that they are using the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  as an inflation-fighting tool," said Sean Callow, a strategist with Westpac Bank.   \par \par \uc2  "This encourages expectations dollar/China will be substantially lower in the coming months, and it's a vote of confidence that most dollar/Asian pairs will be heading lower."   \par \par \uc2  Even if there was a resurgence in market volatility in the coming days, for instance if the Federal Reserve refrained from cutting rates as expected next week, this medium term trend in the Asians would persist, Callow said.   \par \par \uc2  STOCKS RECOVER   \par \par \uc2  The Singapore dollar <SGD=> rose by 0.7 percent to hit a 10-1/2-year high at 1.4205 per dollar, helped by a 3.6 percent rally in its stock market <.FTSTI> as well as the country's close ties to China.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was close to the top of its policy band, and suspected the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curtail further gains.   \par \par \uc2  The ringgit <MYR=> rose 0.9 percent to hit a decade high at 3.2350 per dollar as local stocks <.KLSE> rose by 1.8 percent.   \par \par \uc2  "Money is coming into the market to buy local stocks and bonds," said a trader in Kuala Lumpur.   \par \par \uc2  The broad appreciation in Asia was furthered by firm global equity markets, which in turn were buoyed by a return of some confidence in the U.S economy after solid employment data and a congressional fiscal package, coming just days after the Fed's hefty 75 basis points rate cut.   \par \par \uc2  The Bush administration's fiscal package includes $150 billion of tax rebates and business incentives meant to prevent a slowdown in the country's economy. [ID:nN24242910]   \par \par \uc2  MSCI's measure of Asian equities excluding Japan [.MIAPJ0000PUS] gained more than 5 percent, after overnight gains in the Dow Jones index <.DJI>.   \par \par \uc2  The Japanese yen <JPY=>, borrowed widely as a cheap funding \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  for higher-yielding investments, fell in a sign market participants were gradually renewing their interest in risky trades.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0806 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       107.70         106.33       -1.27\par  Sing dlr        1.4212         1.4312       +0.70\par  Taiwan dlr      32.293         32.352       +0.18\par  Korean won      946.40         949.