\par\par \b \uc2 DJ  \highlight22\uc2 Singapore \highlight\uc2  Dollar Ends Higher, Capped By  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Concerns   \b0\par\par\uc2 380 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Januar 2008\par 09:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.4280   -0.0010 \par    Overnight rate        1.31%    Unchanged \par    Two-year bond yield:  1.32%    -5 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   2.22%    -2 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  1.98%    -2 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   2.92%    -8 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   94 bps   -6 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar finished slightly higher in Asian trade Wednesday, as pressure from the continued decline of the U.S. dollar was tempered by concerns of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The greenback continued to slide on economic concerns and expectations the U.S. Federal Reserve will cut interest rates by another 50 basis points by the end of January. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  slipped to a two-and-half year low against the Japanese yen to Y106.25 and a record low against the Swiss franc of CHF1.0839. But traders said the U.S. dollar is well-supported around the S$1.4250-S$1.4260 range, with Singapore's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  determined to keep the rate of appreciation gradual. The market tested the S$1.4260 support level in midday trading before fears of further MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  pushed the greenback back up. Traders suspect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday, when the U.S. unit fell to a 10-year low of S$1.4257. 'Although the bias is to the downside, there's always caution about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ,' a local trader said. The trader said future market movement will depend on data from the U.S., such as the December consumer price index and the November treasury international capital inflows report tonight. But the trader said the greenback's depreciation will continue, and the market will soon test S$1.4240 with resistance at S$1.4310. Local bond prices continued to rise as investors moved away from the weak equities markets. 'The stock markets of the world aren't doing very well as a general trend,' a local trader said. But the trader said the yield curve will begin to flatten as investors start selling long-term bonds to cash out of the market. -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4155; vincent.lee@dowjones.com -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020080116e41g003pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
