\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Ends Flat; Awaits Cues From US Econ Data   \b0\par\par\uc2 281 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 15 Januar 2008\par 09:01\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.4290   -0.0004 \par    Overnight rate        1.31%    +6 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  1.38%    -6 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   2.26%    -1 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  2.00%    -4 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   3.00%    -6 bps \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   100 bps  -2 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar finished almost unchanged in Asian trade Tuesday, as investors kept to the sidelines awaiting cues from U.S. economic data overnight. Traders said worries about \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore central bank intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the event of a sharp spike in the local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the greenback also kept investors at bay. 'There was nothing much happening today, but sentiment against the (U.S.) dollar remains bearish given worries of a U.S. recession,' said a trader with a foreign bank. Overnight, the U.S. is set to release December retail sales and producer price data, which should give further indications of the likelihood of a recession. 'Also, eyes will be on the results from some U.S. companies that are due later tonight,' said another trader with a local bank. In the local bond market, prices of short-term paper rose as investors continued shifting to safer havens away from volatile stock markets. But overnight rates rose after the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore withdrew S$2.6 billion from the market. 'They are withdrawing excess liquidity in the market after \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market last week,' said a trader with a local bank. -By P.R. Venkat, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 64154 152; venkat.pr@dowjones.com -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020080115e41f003h1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
