\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Flat On MAS Concerns, Weak Asian Equities   \b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Januar 2008\par 09:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2008, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Latest   Change \par    USD/SGD               1.4294   -0.0005 \par    Overnight rate        1.25%    +6 bps \par    Two-year bond yield:  1.48%    -3 bps \par    10-year bond yield:   2.29%    -3 bps \par    Two-year swap offer:  2.04%    -6 bps \par    10-year swap offer:   3.06%    -1 bp \par    2-10-yr swap curve:   102 bps  +5 bps \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar was little changed in Asian trade Monday, with the market cautious due to concerns of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Meanwhile, the U.S. dollar continued its fall against major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as the U.S. Federal Reserve is expected to cut interest rates by 50 basis points when it meets later in January. But traders said prospects of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the MAS are holding them back. On Friday, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  stepped in to slow the appreciation of the Singapore dollar after the greenback fell to a 10-year-low of S$1.4257. 'Like other central banks in the region, they do step in to slow down the volatility,' a local trader said. Weakness among Asian bourses along with Wall Street's selloff Friday has also diminished investors' risk appetite, another local trader said. This has damped demand for the Singapore dollar, leaving the pair to tread water in Monday's session. But traders said the greenback's fall will likely continue, as a series of economic data to be released in the U.S. this week could expose further weakness in the economy. Local bonds continued to rise after big gains Friday. Continued buying interest on the short-term papers led to a further steepening of the yield curve. 'On the back of U.S. Treasurys, Singapore government papers continue on a price increase,' a local trader said, noting that investors were fleeing the languishing equities markets. -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4155; vincent.lee@dowjones.com -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020080114e41e0035y\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
