\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Stocks, Dlr Hit By  \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  Report   \b0\par\par\uc2 622 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2008\par 08:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stocks turned lower in afternoon trade Friday, after a report that  \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  is set to disclose further losses due to the U.S. subprime mortgage crisis soured fragile sentiment.   \par \par \uc2 Shares in Hong Kong were among those hardest hit, with the Hang Seng Index recently down 1.3% after spending much of the day in positive territory.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the Nikkei 225 ended down 1.9%, and near 19-month lows, while South Korea's Kospi Composite lost 2.3%. The S&P/ASX 200 in Australia fell 1.6%.   \par \par \uc2 Markets had started the day on the back foot, after a profit warning from American Express Co. cast a shadow over earlier comments from Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke indicating that the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to deliver bigger rate cuts to ward off an increasing chance of recession.   \par \par \uc2 The renewed focus on the Fed's next interest rate move, and expectations of even weaker yields on the U.S. dollar, was the main driver for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading. The dollar has given up early gains against the yen, and is at multi-year lows against several other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , although it has fared better against Asia-Pacific's barometers of risk aversion, the higher-yielding Australian and New Zealand dollars.   \par \par \uc2 The drops in Asian stocks come despite gains on Wall Street overnight, which saw the Dow Jones Industrial Average rise 117.78 points, or 0.9%, to 12,853.09. While Asian markets earlier seemed to be weighing the advantages of rate cuts against the threat of a U.S. recession, the  \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  news seemed to tip them over the edge.   \par \par \uc2 The New York Times reported that  \uc2 Merrill Lynch\uc2  is expected to suffer $15 billion in losses stemming from soured mortgage investments, almost double its original estimate.   \par \par \uc2 The company is expected to disclose the huge write-down when it reports earnings next week, the paper said, citing people who have been briefed on Merrill's plans.   \par \par \uc2 Not all markets have been hit, though. Extending their recent immunity from financial troubles in the U.S., shares in India remain strong, with the Sensex up 1.1% and buyers targeting banks in particular. In Malaysia, stocks again hit record highs.   \par \par \uc2 Still, a cautious tone is likely to dominate Asia's stock markets for some time, said Khiem Do, head of Asian multi asset at  \uc2 Barings Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 Not only are concerns about technology stocks weighing on tech-heavy markets like Korea and Taiwan, but "the more cynical commentators and investors believe that the Fed and the U.S. government are too late in easing policies in order to avoid a recession," he said. That's keeping investors hesitant.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, meanwhile are also turbulent. The dollar was recently trading as low as Y108.64, after earlier having risen to a high of Y109.71. But, following declines in Korean shares, the dollar has strengthened against the Korean won, to recently trade at KRW937.50.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, though, dollar weakness prompted the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  and buy the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to traders, after it fell to levels not seen since 1997 against the Singapore dollar. The dollar was also at its weakest since 1997 against the Malaysian ringgit.   \par \par \uc2 Gold prices reacted to the falling dollar by returning to a record high in early Asian trading Friday. Gold was recently off that record, but up $2.45 a troy ounce from the close of trading in New York to $893.35 and within reach of the $900 mark.   \par \par \uc2 -By Mohammed Hadi; Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4147; mohammed.hadi@dowjones.com [ 11-01-08 0742GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080111e41b000ai\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
