\par\par \b \uc2 Asia Stks Volatile; Mkt Weighs Rate Cuts Vs Weaker US Econ   \b0\par\par\uc2 873 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 Januar 2008\par 03:57\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stock markets are volatile Friday, as investors weigh the greater prospect of interest rate cuts in the U.S. against fears over the impact of a weakening U.S. economy.   \par \par \uc2 The focus on interest rate policy, and expectations of even weaker yields on the U.S. dollar, is also driving trading in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. The dollar was recently stronger against the yen after a drop in overnight trading, but remains lower against several other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Among stocks, shares in Australia are faring particularly poorly, as cautious statements from U.S. credit card issuer American Express Co. (AMEX) prompt concerns about the country's banks. The S&P/ASX 200 is down 1% to 6015.2, with financial stocks among the worst hit.   \par \par \uc2 "I think that investors gave the Australian banking system the benefit of the doubt, but this benefit of the doubt is being questioned," said Khiem Do, head of Asian Multi-Asset at  \uc2 Baring Asset Management Ltd.\par \par \uc2 Resources stocks, which dominate the Australian stock market, are also hurting on worries about the impact of the slowing U.S. economy on commodity prices. Overnight, crude oil prices slipped on fears that a U.S. recession would crimp demand.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, shares in Japan and South Korea have erased early gains, though expectations for further interest rate cuts in the U.S. are driving shares higher in Hong Kong, where the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  maintains a peg to the dollar and automatically follow U.S. rate moves.   \par \par \uc2 The Kospi Composite in Korea was recently down 0.2% to 1821.67, and the Nikkei 225 in Japan was off 0.8% to 14,272.38. The Hang Seng Index in Hong Kong was up 0.3% to 27,319.41.   \par \par \uc2 But Baring's Do notes that a cautious tone is likely to dominate Asia's stock markets for some time. Not only are concerns about technology stocks weighing on tech-heavy markets like Korea and Taiwan, but "the more cynical commentators and investors believe that the Fed and the U.S. government are too late in easing policies in order to avoid a recession," he said. That's keeping investors hesitant.   \par \par \uc2 Indeed, traders expect markets to remain volatile as investors seek more clarity on the outlook for the U.S. economy and interest rates.   \par \par \uc2 The Straits Times Index in Singapore was recently up 0.8%. "I think we could bounce around a bit like the Nikkei, but basically I think we'll be heading sideways for the next couple of days," a trader in Singapore said.   \par \par \uc2 The volatility follows a mixed bag of news from the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 Shares rose on Wall Street overnight after Federal Reserve Chairman Ben Bernanke said that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is prepared to cut interest rates in response to weakening economic conditions. Also bolstering U.S. markets was word that  \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  is in talks to buy troubled U.S. mortgage lender  \uc2 Countrywide Financial.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average gained 117.78 points, or 0.9%, to 12,853.09.   \par \par \uc2 But Asian traders have more to consider. After U.S. trading ended, U.S. credit card issuer  \uc2 American Express\uc2  said it is seeing signs of a weaker U.S. economy and noted slowing spending by cardholders and an upturn in delinquencies and loan write-offs, sending its stock sharply lower.   \par \par \uc2 Trading in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, meanwhile, reflects the disparity in interest rate policy among central bankers in the U.S. and Asia.   \par \par \uc2 Fed-funds futures traded in the U.S. are now pricing in an 88% chance that the Fed will cut interest rates by 50 basis points at its next meeting.   \par \par \uc2 The dovish comments offered by Bernanke, as well as market expectations on rate cuts, make an "overall environment that should bode positively" for immediate Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading, said  \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  in a note.   \par \par \uc2 Indeed, with the Bank of Korea leaving its interest rate targets unchanged, the Korean Won strengthened against the dollar. The dollar was recently trading at 934.20 won, down 3.30. The dollar was also weaker against the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar, prompting the \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  there to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , according to traders, and has fallen to its lowest level since 1997 against the Malaysian ringgit.   \par \par \uc2 One exception is the yen, which is giving back some overnight gains against the dollar as bargain hunters step in. The dollar was recently up slightly at Y109.50, from an overnight low of 109.13 following Bernanke's comments.   \par \par \uc2 Gold prices reacted to the falling dollar by returning to a record high in early Asian trading Friday. Gold was recently off that record, but up $3.65 a troy ounce from the close of trading in New York to $894.55.   \par \par \uc2 Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney, said: "The reason for (gold's strength) is the weaker dollar...primarily on the back of Bernanke's comments."   \par \par \uc2 Heathcote said he expects gold to push above $900 Friday, although the market will likely wait until London or New York trading to break that historic mark.   \par \par \uc2 -By Mohammed Hadi; Dow Jones Newswires; 65-6415-4147; mohammed.hadi@dowjones.com [ 11-01-08 0257GMT ]   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080111e41b0002p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
