\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Asian Stocks Fall; Oil Prices Raise Inflation Worry   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,116 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 3 Januar 2008\par 05:31\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2008 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Asian stocks are lower Thursday as investors worry about the impact of high energy prices and a weakening U.S. economy after crude prices touched a key threshold overnight.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile the Japanese yen remains firm amid the risk-averse market mood with the Singapore dollar hitting a 10-year high against the U.S. dollar. Other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , like the Taiwan dollar, are weaker against the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Markets are taking their cue from crude oil after it touched $100 a barrel for the first time ever on Wednesday. February Nymex crude ended $3.64, or 3.8%, higher at $99.62 a barrel in New York, and was recently down 31 cents in electronic trade on Globex at $99.31.   \par \par \uc2 The move to $100 is largely symbolic, particularly given it was the result of a single trade, but it has prompted analysts to revisit the question of just how high oil prices might climb and what that could do to economic growth around the globe.   \par \par \uc2 For now, the consensus seems to be that the factors supporting crude prices will be intact for the foreseeable future. Some are very bullish with CLSA analyst Gordon Kwan saying oil may rise to $150 a barrel in 2008, possibly as soon as the next three months.   \par \par \uc2 For traders in other markets, the concern is high oil will create inflationary pressure at a time the U.S. economy is showing signs of weakness, raising talk the U.S. may yet face a period of stagflation. Inflation also creates a problem for Asia's central bankers, who are pinned between concerns about economic growth and suppressing prices.   \par \par \uc2 Beyond that is the risk $100-a-barrel oil will weigh on consumer confidence - in part via higher gasoline prices.   \par \par \uc2 Crude's next move may rest on the release later Thursday of weekly U.S. inventory data, with a Dow Jones poll of six analysts expecting an average drawdown in crude stocks of 1.8 million barrels.   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, spot gold hit a record high of $861.15 a troy ounce, taking out the previous peak of $852 in 1980, helped by a weaker U.S. dollar. Recently gold was $1.50 lower from the overnight close.   \par \par \uc2 Soft commodity prices have also gained, led by palm oil, a biofuel feedstock that can substitute for crude.   \par \par \uc2 Palm oil opened at an all-time high on the Bursa Malaysia Derivatives exchange of MYR3,159 a metric ton for March delivery, before slipping back a touch. The March contract is now MYR50 higher on the day at MYR3,132/ton.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    More Cues Sought On US Economy \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders are now looking to Friday's release of a monthly non-farm payrolls report in the U.S. for further leads on the economy, after data Thursday showed the Institute of Supply Management's December manufacturing index fell to 47.7 from 50.8 in November, the weakest reading in nearly five years and an indication of contraction in the manufacturing sector of the economy.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The reaction to the much weaker than expected ISM indicator shows how sensitive the market is to negative news out of the U.S.," said Standard Chartered in a research note. "We do believe there are significant downside risks to U.S. domestic demand and the global credit markets in the coming quarters, which will be felt globally."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stocks are tracking Wall Street's decline, though liquidity remains thin with Japanese markets closed until Friday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The S&P/ASX 200 in Australia has fallen 1.4% to 6,263.7, while the Kospi Composite in South Korea is off 0.8% to 1,839.24. Losses in Korea are limited as the market already fell 2.3% Wednesday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Shares in Hong Kong are also faring poorly with the Hang Seng down 1.7% to 27,089.41, and the Straits Times Index in Singapore off 1.5% to 3,409.84.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among market sectors, gold, oil and alternative energy shares are faring well, while companies that rely heavily on energy faring poorly.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Korean petrochemical companies are lower as analysts say these companies will find it difficult to pass on higher input costs as quickly as oil prices are rising. LG Chem (051910.SE) was recently down 4.5%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among alternative energy companies, Taiwan's Gintech Energy (3514.TW) was up 1.3%, and E-Ton Solar Tech (3452.OT) up 1.7%, while Korean solar energy companies are also on the rise with DC Chemical (010060.SE) up 2.6%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil-sensitive airline shares are generally weaker, though some airlines may benefit from a weaker U.S. dollar, as it cuts the cost of funding their dollar-denominated debt.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rise in gold prices has aided shares of gold miners across the region. China's Shandong Gold-Mining (600547.SH) has gained 8.5%, while in Australia, Newcrest (NCM.AU) has added 5%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar is still weak against Japanese yen after an overnight tumble, recently around Y109.55.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile the Singapore dollar reached a ten year high against the U.S. dollar, with traders watching for signs of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Monetary Authority\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Singapore\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts generally look for further dollar declines in the near term. "The trend will be for a weak dollar over the first few months of the year," said  \f28 \uc2 \f2 Citigroup\f28 \uc2 \f2  economist Stephen Halmarick.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, CMC Markets' chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  analyst Ashraf Laidi looks for a dollar recovery going into 2008, and adds this can come in tandem with prolonged gold gains.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Global interest rates are likely to head lower with investors hit by a double whammy of rising inflation and falling nominal yields, "two reasons prompting the shift to higher-yielding gold," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the same time, U.S. interest rate cuts won't occur in a vacuum, while prolonged selling in equities should trigger falls in higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  against the dollar, he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond markets are rising after a gain in U.S. Treasurys overnight, with bonds taking their cue from the poor ISM reading.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Mohammed Hadi, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4147, mohammed.hadi@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Rosalind Mathieson, Dow Jones Newswires; +65-6415-4141, rosalind.mathieson@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 03-01-08 0431GMT ]   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020080103e4130002t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
