\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Ends Up; Bonds Fall On Strong Inflation Data   \b0\par\par\uc2 333 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Dezember 2007\par 08:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Latest   Change\par    USD/SGD               1.4518   -0.0015\par    Overnight rate        0.69%    Unchanged\par    Two-year bond yield:  1.80%    +4 bps\par    10-year bond yield:   2.68     +2 bps\par    Two-year swap offer:  2.72%    +2 bps\par    10-year swap offer:   3.39%    +4 bps\par    2-10-yr swap curve:   67 bps   +2 bps\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar ended slightly higher on thin volume Wednesday in Asian trade, with few traders around and the market having priced in domestic inflationary pressures. 'The Singapore dollar has been quite flat in the past few trading sessions on thin trading,' a strategist with a local bank said. 'Not a lot of traders are around, and there's not a lot of trading interest.' Although Singapore's November consumer price index data - released Monday - showed the highest rate of inflation in 25 years at 4.2% on year, the strategist said the upward movement was already priced in by the market. Another trader said the market is wary of another \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which seeks a gradual appreciation of the domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  against the U.S. greenback. The strategist said the U.S. dollar should close the year around S$1.45, but said the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  will continue to slide next year on an anticipated slowdown in the world's largest economy and expectations of further rate cuts by the U.S. Federal Reserve. The strategist also said that continued international pressure on the Chinese government to let the yuan appreciate faster will serve to strengthen most other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the local bond market, trading was thin, with short-term yields moving up on selling pressure following the domestic inflation data. A local trader said that most market players were closing their positions ahead of the year-end. -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4154; vincent.lee@dowjones.com -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020071226e3cq002e5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
