\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Ends Flat; Held Back By Fear Of  \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 332 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 Dezember 2007\par 09:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Latest   Change\par    USD/SGD               1.4422   +0.0003\par    Overnight rate        1.19%    +19bps\par    Two-year bond yield:  1.82%    -8 bps\par    10-year bond yield:   2.81%    -3 bps\par    Two-year swap offer:  2.78%    -4 bps\par    10-year swap offer:   3.48%    -4bps\par    2-10-yr swap curve:   70 bpsUnchanged\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)-- The Singapore dollar finished flat in Asian trade Wednesday despite a selloff on Wall Street, as traders remained cautious after suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  the previous day. On Tuesday, the U.S. Federal Reserve cut interest rates by 25 basis points to 4.25%. Wall Street, however, did not think the cut was big enough, resulting in a 2.1% drop in the Dow Jones Industrial Average to 13,432.77 points. A local trader said support for the U.S. dollar should hold around S$1.44 for the near term, as the rate cut was already priced in and investors weren't anxious to challenge the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "All the few dips we've seen below 1.44 have failed," the trader said. "People are just a little bit cautious on the downside." The trader said there were concerns about the possibility of further \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which was suspected to have entered the market Tuesday when the greenback hit a low of S$1.4372. But traders said the Singapore dollar is likely to continue to appreciate over time along with other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . One local trader said the Singapore \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has no choice but to allow the appreciation due to inflation. Local government bond prices rose in late trade on buying by foreign investors, especially in short-to-medium terms, to park the Singapore dollars they have been buying in anticipation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's further appreciation. -By Se Young Lee, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4154; vincent.lee@dowjones.com -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020071212e3cc003pe\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
