\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Central banks step in as Asians reach for highs   \b0\par\par\uc2 844 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 Oktober 2007\par 05:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 26 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar, Korean won and Malaysian ringgit hit fresh 10-year highs on Friday after weak U.S. data led to renewed selling of the dollar and spurred some Asian central banks to step into the market.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar fell broadly after Thursday's soft durable goods orders, coming on top of the previous day's data showing existing home sales at a record low in September, which intensified expectations of another rate cut by the Federal Reserve next week.   \par \par \uc2 U.S. crude oil prices at $92 a barrel and the jump in gold prices to 28-year highs also hurt the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar hit a record low of $1.4377 against the euro <EUR=>.   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> was capped at a 10-year high of 1.4523 per U.S. dollar, after a rally of a quarter percent. It retreated in later deals to 1.4595, but analysts remained bullish.   \par \par \uc2 "I would sell rallies on the U.S. dollar whenever the MAS \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Inflation is creeping higher, so the Sing dollar will eventually appreciate," said a trader.   \par \par \uc2 That seemed to be the predominant view.   \par \par \uc2 BNP Paribas analyst Thio Chin Loo said she sees the Singapore dollar at 1.40 by the year-end.   \par \par \uc2 JPMorgan's strategists said Asian central banks were likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rein in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  but would progressively have to buy dollars at lower levels since bad news from credit markets and views on U.S. rates would weigh on the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Singapore dollar had outperformed some of its neighbours and was near the top end of the secret trade-weighted band in which the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems very plausible to me," said Westpac Bank's Sean Callow, who felt that long positions in the Indian rupee <INR=IN> and Philippine peso had better potential for returns.   \par \par \uc2 RINGGIT, PESO   \par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> hit 3.345 per dollar, rising past the 3.35 level for the first time since late 1997 and taking its gains in just 3 sessions to more than 1 percent.   \par \par \uc2 Traders said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had bought dollars through some banks, but the momentum of ringgit-buying was too strong.   \par \par \uc2 Suresh Kumar Ramanathan of CIMB Bank said the ringgit would see a sudden jump if the 3.345 level was breached, with the next resistance coming in only at 3.33 - a level seen in Nov. 1997.   \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the day, Malaysian Trade Minister Rafidah Aziz was quoted by media as telling businesses they should learn to cope with ringgit fluctuations even if it worked against them.   \par \par \uc2 The Philippine \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BSP) was suspected of buying dollars to keep the peso <PHP=> from hitting the 44-per-dollar level. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  stalled at a seven-year high of 44.02 per dollar, just a fraction higher than Thursday's 44.03 peak.   \par \par \uc2 "BSP is buying dollars heavily but the market flows are all on one side -- selling," a trader in Manila said.   \par \par \uc2 South Korea's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warned market participants it would take steps if the won <KRW=> rose excessively. The won stopped short of 909 per dollar, but hit 10-year highs.   \par \par \uc2 The Japanese yen <JPY=> was fickle on Friday, rising initially on the dollar's weakness to near 114 per dollar and then falling as the Nikkei average <.N225> and other Asian stock markets <0#.INDEXA> rose and investors seemed to be selectively returning to risky assets.   \par \par \uc2 MSCI's measure of Asia Pacific stocks excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> hit a record high of 572.20, surpassing the previous peak of 569.13 set on Oct. 11.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at 0804 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.42         114.23       -0.17\par  Sing dlr        1.4557         1.4576       +0.13\par  Taiwan dlr      32.456         32.475       +0.06\par  Korean won      909.80         916.40       +0.73\par  Baht             34.07          34.13       +0.18\par  Peso             44.05          44.04       -0.05\par  Rupiah         9115.00        9163.00       +0.53\par  Rupee            39.48          39.51       +0.07\par  Ringgit         3.3480         3.3550       +0.21\par  Yuan            7.4870         7.4822       -0.06\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.42         119.01       +4.01\par  Sing dlr        1.4557         1.5337       +5.36\par  Taiwan dlr      32.456         32.585       +0.40\par  Korean won      909.80         929.50       +2.17\par  Baht             34.07          36.08       +5.90\par  Peso             44.05          49.03      +11.28\par  Rupiah         9115.00        8988.00       -1.39\par  Rupee            39.48          44.26      +12.09\par  Ringgit         3.3480         3.5270       +5.35\par  Yuan            7.4870         7.8051       +4.25\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 MARKETS-ASIA-\b \highl