\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia dithers on listless stocks, U.S. dollar   \b0\par\par\uc2 917 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 24 Oktober 2007\par 05:13\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 24 (Reuters) - The Singapore dollar recovered a quarter of a percent on Wednesday but other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  were listless, trapped in tight ranges by the dollar's vacillations and uncertainty about risk appetite.   \par \par \uc2 Asian stock markets also wavered, hurt in later trades by reports that  \uc2 Merrill Lynch & Co.\uc2  was expected to add more losses related to declining market values for its debt holdings.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar slipped overnight but clawed back in Asian trading against the euro <EUR=> and a broad basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as markets waited for Wednesday's U.S. existing home sales data.   \par \par \uc2 But it has been volatile this week, falling after the Group of Seven officials failed to explicitly mention its weakness, rising subsequently from what some considered oversold levels on Monday and weakening again on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 The yen <JPY=> rallied, indicating another bout of risk aversion as investors exited carry trades.   \par \par \uc2 In emerging Asia, the Singapore dollar <SGD=> rose to 1.4614 per U.S. dollar, and the Malaysian ringgit rose 0.2 percent to 3.3670 per dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The South Korean won <KRW=> fell to 918 per dollar after an initial 0.1 percent rise to 916.6 per dollar, in spite of verbal and suspected dollar-buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Korean authorities in the previous session.   \par \par \uc2 "Investors are still cautious on Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the trend has not been as strong or defined as the equity markets," said Claudio Piron, a strategist at JPMorgan Chase Bank.   \par \par \uc2 The Indian rupee <INR=IN> rose to 39.455 per dollar, then pared gains as the stock market <.BSESN> fell.   \par \par \uc2 The rupee has, however, rebounded sharply from lows of 39.96 struck last Wednesday after regulators proposed restrictions on derivatives used by foreigners investing in the Indian market. The measures could be implemented on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 CHINA SPECULATION   \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the non-deliverable forwards <CNYNDFOR=> market showed the yuan at fresh highs after media quoted an in-house report by the National Development and Reform Commission as suggesting China should consider a one-off 15-20 percent yuan revaluation.   \par \par \uc2 A news department official at NDRC said he was unaware of this report, but it gave markets the ammunition to speculate further on the possibility China will allow the tightly-managed yuan to rise faster.   \par \par \uc2 Spot yuan <CNY=CFXS> hit 7.4928 per dollar, its highest since the yuan was revalued in July 2005. One-year NDFs took the yuan to the firmer side of 7-per-dollar for the first time, to 6.996 or a 7.1 percent effective appreciation in 12 months.   \par \par \uc2 "With the implied yuan 12-month gain now sitting just short of 7 percent, either the recent speculative selling is an inspired bet on an imminent yuan surge or it is time to buy NDFs on a range-play heading back to a more typical 5 percent handle," Westpac Bank's Sean Callow said in a note.   \par \par \uc2 Callow said he would bet on the latter, but added that sentiment on the dollar would have to improve considerably for NDFs to move back to being less bullish yuan.   \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the fear of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar bulls from chasing the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still some way off 10-year highs of 1.4577 struck last week, when the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  (MAS) was suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold it down. It has moved between 1.46 and 1.4678 this week.   \par \par \uc2 "Eventually, Sing dollar has to rise but, in the short term, there's MAS to deal with," a trader in Hong Kong said.   \par \par \uc2 Westpac Bank's Callow said that while the MAS's policy of encouraging \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  appreciation to rein in inflation reinforced the firm tone for the Singapore dollar, it had still outperformed trading partners such as the yuan and rupiah in recent weeks.   \par \par \uc2 "Hence while the Singapore dollar does have the appeal of less vulnerability to passing global jitters, there is limited prospect for returns on short U.S. dollar/Singapore dollar to beat the U.S. dollar/Asia average since MAS bids won't be far away," he said in a note.    \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCIES\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  VS U.S. DOLLAR  Change on the day at   0817 GMT\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid   Previous day    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.76         114.51       -0.22\par  Sing dlr        1.4616         1.4650       +0.23\par  Taiwan dlr      32.559         32.580       +0.06\par  Korean won      916.80         917.90       +0.12\par  Baht             34.16          34.15       -0.03\par  Peso             44.05          44.06       +0.02\par  Rupiah         9127.00        9135.00       +0.09\par  Rupee            39.59          39.64       +0.11\par  Ringgit         3.3680         3.3730       +0.15\par  Yuan            7.4937         7.5062       +0.17\par  Change so far in 2007\par  \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2     Latest bid  End prev year    Pct Move\par  Japan yen       114.76         119.01