\par\par \b \uc2 DJ Singapore Dollar Ended Slightly Higher; Gains Limited   \b0\par\par\uc2 375 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 22 Oktober 2007\par 12:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Chinese Financial Wire\par \uc2 DJCFWE\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright (c) 2007, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par                          Latest   Change\par    USD/SGD               1.4615   -0.0030\par    Overnight rate        2.25%    -25 bps\par    Two-year bond yield:  1.88%    -2 bps\par    10-year bond yield:   2.75%    -2 bps\par    Two-year swap offer:  2.75%    -3 bps\par    10-year swap offer:   3.23%    -1 bps\par    2-10-yr swap curve:   48 bps   +2 bps\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--The Singapore dollar ended slightly higher Monday but gains were limited by general risk aversion across \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. 'The Singapore dollar is still attractive but if you look at the carry-trade unwinding, it's clear the market is uneasy,' a trader said. A statement by the G7 in Washington Friday called for renewed attention on the risks to global growth, causing many players to trim speculative positions. The U.S. dollar dropped as low as S$1.4610 in early trade, but reversed course in a sudden rise later in the morning session to S$1.4660. '(The Singapore dollar) could only get so far before risk-aversion came into play,' the trader said. Investors will also be wary of building long positions in the local dollar after \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  Friday to stem its rise. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  manages the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  within a trade-weighted band, and models at many investment banks indicate the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is near the top of the undisclosed range. One trader said the greenback has scope to fall below S$1.4600, but a drop to S$1.4550 could prompt another move by the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 . In the local bond market, prices surged in morning trade as the 2008 issuance calendar revealed a disappointing supply lineup. Friday's surge in U.S. Treasurys also drove local paper higher, with the yield on Singapore's benchmark 2-year bond falling to its lowest level since February 2005. But the 2-year benchmark may stabilize in coming sessions as the payout is well below the overnight funding cost. 'The negative carry won't stay so big,' a trader said. 'There's definitely room for the 2-year to correct after such a move (in the bond).' -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4153; john.jannarone@dowjones.com -0-    \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCFWE0020071022e3am004s9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
