\par\par \b \uc2 2nd UPDATE: Singapore MAS Tightens Policy As Inflation Picks Up   \b0\par\par\uc2 1,067 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 Oktober 2007\par 06:44\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    (Adds market response, economists' comments, additional background.) \par  \par    By John Jannarone \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 SINGAPORE (Dow Jones)--Singapore shifted monetary policy a notch tighter Wednesday in response to rising inflationary pressure and an upbeat economic growth outlook, a surprise move marking the first change in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's stance in over three years.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said it would increase slightly the slope of its undisclosed trade-weighted \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  band but that it hadn't changed the width or centering of the policy target.   \par \par \uc2 The news caused the Singapore dollar to jump to a 10-year high as investors speculated the authorities are now willing to let the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise at a faster pace to temper increases in consumer prices.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. dollar fell to S$1.4631 from S$1.4720 just before the announcement, putting traders on guard for possible \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities seeking to smooth sharp movements. The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  later rebounded to just above S$1.4650, amid unconfirmed rumors that the MAS had entered the market.   \par \par \uc2 The monetary tightening suggests that Singapore policy makers are serious about getting inflation under control, even at the expense of slower growth. The MAS's more hawkish stance led some analysts to predict the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  might tighten policy again as soon as its next meeting in April.   \par \par \uc2 The decision also highlights the need for Asian central banks to tailor policy to suit local economic conditions, even as the Federal Reserve recently moved to an easier money policy. For Singapore, which uses the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate to influence prices because external trade dwarfs the domestic economy, that means that a tighter monetary policy - or stronger Singapore dollar - is warranted.   \par \par \uc2 "In Singapore the business cycle is not exactly in synch with the U.S., so there's a need for fine-tuning," said Chua Hak Bin, an economist at  \uc2 Citigroup\uc2  in Singapore.   \par \par \uc2 Wednesday's move marked the first time since April 2004 that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore has altered its monetary policy. Since then, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  had kept a preference for a modest and gradual appreciation of the Singapore dollar, paving the way for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to rise 16% against the U.S. dollar over that period.   \par \par \uc2 Behind the change in stance is concern that inflation is starting to become a problem for the city state, analysts said.   \par \par \uc2 Inflation in Singapore may "have breached the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's comfort zone," said Chua, noting the MAS expects the consumer price index to rise by 3.5% in the first half of 2008.   \par \par \uc2 "Inflation is going to ease back, but it will be near a historical high," he said.   \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said inflation will average between 2%-3% in 2008 after coming in at 1.5%-2% in 2007.   \par \par \uc2 A recent increase in Singapore's sales tax is partly to blame for the higher rate of inflation. The MAS estimates that 0.7 percentage point of each year's rise reflects a one-time impact of the tax hike to 7% from 5% on July 1.   \par \par \uc2 But there is reason to believe that price pressures have deeper roots. The MAS warned that prices for food, oil and other commodities are expected to continue to pick up, while higher property prices will also begin to feed into consumer inflation.   \par \par \uc2 Private property prices in Singapore are still below the peak from 1996, but the local real estate market continues to boom with few signs of a slowdown.   \par \par \uc2 Third-quarter private property prices rose 8% from the second quarter after rising 8.3% in the previous three months. The second quarter increase was the fastest in eight years.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Broad-Based Expansion \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Robust economic growth is underpinning the real estate boom and a tight labor market.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The economy grew 6.4% in the third quarter from the second on an adjusted, annualized basis, led by strength across the economy and a faster expansion in manufacturing, Singapore's trade ministry said in a separate release.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That was a slower pace than in the second quarter, when gross domestic product expanded 14.4%, but it beat analysts' projections. A Dow Jones Newswires poll of economists forecast the economy had expanded 5.6%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ministry said the economy is "well on track" to meet the government's 7%-8% growth forecast in 2007.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's mainstay manufacturing expanded 12.3% in the third quarter from a year earlier after growing 8.3% in the second quarter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The services sector grew 8.1% from a year earlier after rising 8.4% in the second quarter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The sizzling property market continued to support construction, which jumped 15.5% following a 18.8% expansion in the previous three months.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Compared with a year earlier, the economy grew 9.4%, compared with 8.6% growth in the second quarter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Dow Jones poll pointed to on-year growth of 9.1% in the third quarter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The advance GDP figures are mainly based on data from the first two months of the quarter.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Hawkish Tone \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists say Wednesday's monetary policy tweak sets a precedent for further adjustments if price pressures persist.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We see room for further MAS tightening at the next monetary policy meeting in April 2008," said Matthew Hildebrandt, an economist at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 J.P. Morgan\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 He said Wednesday's statement had "a much more hawkish tone than the last, with concerns about inflation clearly outweighing those about growth."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's statement said the U.S. economy has weakened and other economies are also likely to soften, but Singapore may post economic growth of 4%-6% in 2008.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The global economy is expected to remain resilient, particularly in Asia," the MAS statement said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As the economy expands apace, wage growth has picked up, adding to inflationary pressures.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Wages rose 8.5% from a year earlier in the second quarter, the fastest pace in seven years.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Also, major gauges of money supply show expansion exceeding 20%, the fastest expansion since the early 1990s.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Given the strong growth numbers clearly inflationary pressures are building in the economy," said Prakriti Sofat, an economist at  \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  in Singapore.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We think there are signs of overheating."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -By John Jannarone, Dow Jones Newswires; 65 6415 4153; john.jannarone@dowjones.com \par    -Edited by Arran Scott \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 10-10-07 0444GMT   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020071010e3aa0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
