\par\par \b \uc2 Emerging  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2 -Asia retreats from highs, ringgit falls most   \b0\par\par\uc2 758 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 Oktober 2007\par 05:02\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2007 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 2 (Reuters) - The Malaysian ringgit fell half a percent on Tuesday, leading a retreat in Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  as investors exited stretched positions ahead of a string of U.S. economic data that could determine direction for the U.S. dollar.   \par \par \uc2 The ringgit <MYR=> gave up all of this week's gains to hit 3.4110 per dollar, down 0.8 percent from Monday's 4-month high of 3.3850.   \par \par \uc2 The Singapore dollar <SGD=> shed 0.5 percent, swinging from a high of 1.4753 per U.S. dollar to a low of 1.4835.   \par \par \uc2 And the Indonesian rupiah <IDR=> was forced off a 2-month high of 9,055 per dollar and pushed to levels around 9,100, slightly down on the day.   \par \par \uc2 "I think there was some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Singapore\b0 \highlight\uc2  dollar," said a trader.   \par \par \uc2 "But, in general, people are also worried about the sharp sell-off in dollar/Asia, and some fast money accounts are covering their shorts now," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Worries about excessive positioning against the dollar also saw high-yielders such as the Australian dollar <AUD=> decline.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar stayed just off record lows against the euro <EUR=>, helped in part by a record high close in the Dow Jones industrial average <.DJI> on Monday.   \par \par \uc2 It also got a breather as markets waited for a series of data and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy meetings, including the Bank of England and European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. Institute for Supply Management's non-manufacturing index and non-farm payrolls reports are due later this week, and could offer clues on whether U.S. rates will be cut again to offset the impact of a housing market slump.   \par \par \uc2 The Philippine peso gained 0.2 percent to 44.74 per dollar in early deals before ending local trade slightly weaker at 44.915.   \par \par \uc2 The Taiwan dollar <TWD=TP> retreated to 32.57 from the previous session's 9-month high of 32.465.   \par \par \uc2 Markets in India and China were closed for holidays.   \par \par \uc2 BULLISH RUPIAH   \par \par \uc2 Foreign demand for Indonesian stocks and bonds has helped drive the rupiah up nearly 4 percent in 3 weeks. Some analysts expect it will continue to be strong near term.   \par \par \uc2 The rupiah has been underpinned by investment inflows into a rising Jakarta stock market <.JKSE>, whose index hit an all-time high on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2 Analysts said expectations that the authorities will cut policy rates again and also encourage rupiah appreciation to rein in imported inflation from higher oil prices were behind the growing foreign appetite for Indonesian assets.   \par \par \uc2 Indonesia's one-month policy rate, now at 8.25 percent, has already been cut 13 times in line with falling inflation, but there are signs now that price pressures are returning.   \par \par \uc2 "The broad U.S. dollar backdrop continues to be weak... and the balance of payments surplus will continue to support rupiah appreciation,"  \uc2 JPMorgan Chase\uc2  said in a note.   \par \par \uc2 "This, in our view, will drive dollar/rupiah to 8,700 by year-end and may also dampen inflationary pressures through softer import prices,"  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  said.   \par \par \uc2 But  \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  also warned that low interest rates could spur a domestic demand recovery and that would cause the trade surplus to shrink next year, resulting in a weaker bias for the rupiah. \b \highlight22\uc2 CURRENCIES\b0 \highlight\uc2  VS U.S. D